\f1\fs22 Hours before the High Elvis and Alexander, and half a day before Gerry and his Clown Squad arrived at Sublime Point, two and a half kilometres to the west, while civil twilight was just beginning to pick shapes out of the Jamison Valley, another PAPER unit mobilised. Down nearly a thousand steps, each member of Tiger Squad descended from the plateau to the valley floor carrying two automatic rifles, ammunition, provisions, climbing gear and camping equipment. If they were bushwalkers, they were bushwalkers who had anticipated objections about where they went and where they might arrive.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 They were not bushwalkers. They carried no communications equipment, and made no conversation.\
They descended by ledge and through narrow crevices while the Three Sisters looked down upon them among their petticoats. At times they could look clear across the valley to a solitary mountain stretching east to west like a long, flat-topped wall. Beneath its gumtree plateau, pale sandstone cliffs broke into great blocks above a sweep of eucalyptus falling deep into the valley.\
At the bottom, they followed a path that roughly traced the exposed cliff and led through fallen sandstone wearing thick mossy shawls, and gullies dense with ferns under canopies of coachwood, sassafras and lilly pilly. They passed pools of silvery water replenished by little waterfalls you could almost get your hands around, and through a clearing that seemed designed for picnics where the canopy gave an impression they were in a cathedral whose designers hadn\'92t expected visitors with enough guns to share. \
From there, they turned back uphill beside a creek beneath tree ferns and between blackened stone walls where water settled in shallow pools and followed little cascades that spread over rock in silver sheets. Beneath cliffs, they followed a narrow seam scarcely more than a ledge above the forest falling away below. For long stretches they moved under overhangs and beside walls streaked black by water. Then the trees would open and they could look across to the solitary flat-backed mountain to gauge their progress without the need for instruments.\
At a waterfall they were uncertain of the route around. They picked down over slippery, hazardous rock, crossed under the falls. Beyond, the path narrowed and deteriorated, blocked by fallen trees, and bitten away by small landslips. In places water coursed over the cliff, and there was barely a boot\'92s width among stones. Elsewhere only sandstone, leaf litter and the footfall of the person in front leant any confidence to the next step.\
They passed behind a large waterfall with the cliff at their backs and a running curtain dropping between them and the valley through which they saw sunlight rising on the forest below.\
The wall they had spent most of the day rounding rose directly above them in tiers of sandstone broken by gullies and shelves of fern and scrub. Through openings in the trees they could look back west towards the Three Sisters, and south to the mesa mountain.
\f2\fs24 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \

\f1\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0 A movement among the trees above made the team point raise the signal to hold. Breaths held and eyes scanned. Rifle barrels traced potential hostile positions. Rifle fire cut through the scrub and dropped two of Tiger Squad. One fell where he stood;  the other fell backwards through a stand of dog rose and disappeared down the slope. Those still standing took cover in both directions, against the cliff, behind trees and in the shadows of sandstone. Rounds struck and sent chips of rock and bark through the ferns. A Tiger operative fired uphill from behind a fallen trunk while another crawled back to the one who had gone over the edge. \
Tiger Squad held and waited; the Shakti patrol knew the terrain and had taken the higher ground. Before long, fire started coming at them from different positions. Most of Tiger Squad kept quiet, held position and waited for the patrol to make mistakes in their approach.\
The Shakti patrol tried to move around the lower side of Tiger Squad and ran into two of them who had been driven farther downslope by the opening fire. Another Shakti pair worked back towards the cliff, where one of Tiger Squad had already climbed above the track through a break in the sandstone. The distances shortened between combatants as for several minutes the encounter devolved into smaller disputes. No one could see all of it.\
A Shakti operative appeared from behind a sandstone block where Tiger Squad had not expected anyone. One of Tiger Squad worked through a break in the cliff and got above the Shakti line without detection. The Shakti patrol lost its advantage by degrees, until their positions had splintered, and they began pulling uphill. Allowing the patrol to retreat to the plateau was no option for Tiger Squad, so they went on the hunt.\
A Shakti operative fired from behind a tree until they were driven by fierce fire away from it and picked off. Another continued with a sidearm after losing her rifle, but not for long. One made it uphill towards a sandstone shelf before three members of Tiger Squad found an angle on him. The more the Shakti patrol sought safety and reinforcements, the easier they were to dispatch.\
The firing stopped, but Tiger Squad had no way to know if they\'92d neutralised the whole patrol, if they\'92d got a message off, or what they\'92d find when they reached the plateau.\
Tiger Squad held position and waited for another patrol. None came. They were lucky; Alexander had seized Eloise and Shakti Security was already fighting itself.\
They counted their people.\
Several were dead. Others were wounded badly enough that taking them up the pass was impossible. One could still walk with help but not climb. Another remained conscious while blood continued through a dressing faster than anyone had optimism for.\
They treated the wounded, then searched the dead for weapons, identification and anything they could not leave behind. Bodies were moved away from the path and concealed beneath rock and scrub. The wounded who could not continue were settled together under cover with food, water, medical supplies and weapons. Arrangements were made for their exfiltration.\
Equipment was stripped back and redistributed. The second rifles were redistributed among fewer people, as were the packs. Anything dispensable was  concealed  near the wounded. \
What remained of Tiger Squad turned uphill, and climbed through boulders and clefts towards the sandstone nose of Sublime Point, gaining in a few hundred horizontal metres most of the height they had spent the morning surrendering. Packs came off when crevices narrowed too much. An operative climbed through narrow channels in the rock face so others could pass up rifles, packs and equipment. \
The squad reached an overhang where they rested and sheltered and looked out. After the souring of their adrenalin, the Jamison Valley appeared to be suspended out there like a landscape hung on a crooked nail. \
A little more rested, they climbed through weathered sandstone, then up short near-vertical scrambles of roots  and cracks until they encountered pouched coral fern and dog rose crowded sheltered clefts that thinned abruptly where exposed sandstone opened among heath banksia and grass trees.\
The last scramble lifted them out of fern and shadow onto open sandstone, heath and eucalypt woodland. The cliff they had spent the day under or beside beside was finally beneath their boots. Exhausted, they kept to the bush west of the track and south of the townhouses, and look for a place to encamp and wait.
